---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import release from '../../data/release.json';
---

<PageLayout
  lang="en"
  title="What is ZIGNOS — ZIGNOS"
  description="ZIGNOS is a source-based Linux distribution built from scratch. A summary of its philosophy, how it works, the size of the base, and who it is for."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <span class="eyebrow">ABOUT ZIGNOS</span>
      <h1>What is ZIGNOS</h1>
      <p>
        A source-based Linux distribution built from scratch.
        Not a finished product to use as-is, but a foundation to assemble with your own hands.
      </p>
      <p class="catch">Engineered for Performance. Designed for Independence.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose what">
      <p class="statement">
        Rather than choosing an OS that is handed to you,<br />
        you assemble your own.
      </p>

      <p>
        ZIGNOS is a source-based distribution. Starting from a from-scratch
        build procedure, it builds everything from the toolchain to the base
        system by hand. It is not a derivative of an existing distribution;
        it combines its own package manager <strong>zpkg</strong> with
        <strong>OpenRC</strong> as init.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal is a system you can understand, that contains nothing
        unnecessary, and that stays under your control for a long time.
        No editor, no SSH, no DHCP from the start.
        Only what you need, only as much as you need.
      </p>

      <h2>Why build from scratch</h2>
      <p>
        Distributions have become convenient. In exchange, it has become harder
        to see what runs inside them and where each dependency comes from.
        ZIGNOS goes the other way. It keeps the build process as recipes,
        records everything installed with zpkg, and keeps every choice in your hands.
      </p>

      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <span class="icon">◆</span>
          <h3>Source-based</h3>
          <p>
            Recipes are shell scripts. You choose the build flags and dependencies
            yourself. Built binaries are cached, which makes rebuilds faster.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="icon">◆</span>
          <h3>Minimal base</h3>
          <p>
            The smallest possible configuration. The base is {release.base.top_level} packages,
            or {release.base.closure} with dependencies. Add only what you need from there.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="icon">◆</span>
          <h3>Simple init</h3>
          <p>
            init is OpenRC. systemd is not used. Services are opt-in,
            and you choose what starts.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="icon">◆</span>
          <h3>Rolling release</h3>
          <p>
            The HEAD of each channel (stable / testing / unstable) is the current release.
            <code>zpkg upgrade</code> shows the changes, and you follow them after reviewing.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>What makes up ZIGNOS</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>zpkg</strong> — A package manager written in Go. It has USE flags, slots,
          a binpkg cache, transactions, an install journal, Ed25519 signing,
          and a build sandbox via bubblewrap.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Recipe tree</strong> — Three channels: stable / testing / unstable.
          core is the base system, main is an optional set of CLI tools,
          and desktop covers KDE Plasma 6.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>stage3</strong> — A minimal, chrootable base without the kernel and
          bootloader. Unpack it and finish with <code>zpkg install linux grub</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>BIOS / UEFI</strong> — Supports both firmware types. GRUB selects
          <code>bios</code> / <code>uefi</code> with USE flags, and efibootmgr can also be installed on UEFI.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Developer</strong> — Developed and operated by <a href="https://studio-nosa.live/" rel="external">Studio NØSa.</a>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The base in numbers</h2>
      <div class="stats">
        <div class="stat"><strong>{release.base.closure}</strong><span>Base packages (with dependencies)</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><strong>{release.base.top_level}</strong><span>Top level of base.list</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><strong>{release.recipes.total}</strong><span>Recipes (three channels total)</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><strong>{release.stage3.package_count}</strong><span>Packages in stage3</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
        stage3 is about {release.stage3.size_mib} MiB. It does not include the kernel, DHCP, WiFi, SSH, an editor, or man;
        add what you need with <code>zpkg install</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it is for, and who it is not</h2>
      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Who it is for</h3>
          <p>
            People who like building their environment by hand.
            People who want to work CLI-first, without unnecessary packages.
            People who want to choose only what they need and manage it for the long term.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Who it is not for</h3>
          <p>
            People who want a GUI right after installation. People who want automatic
            updates or a huge binary repository. People who require Secure Boot.
            The first official release was published on 2026.11.24. stage3 does not include a GUI
            (KDE Plasma 6 can be added from the recipes).
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <dl class="faq">
        <dt>How is it different from other source-based distributions?</dt>
        <dd>
          ZIGNOS is not based on Gentoo. It does not use Portage / ebuild / Gentoo
          recipes; it builds everything from the toolchain to the minimal base with
          its own bootstrap procedure, and manages it with its own package manager
          zpkg and recipes. The idea of composing a system from a minimal base and
          recipes is shared, but package management and the build system are
          independent.
        </dd>
        <dt>Is a desktop environment included?</dt>
        <dd>
          stage3 is CLI only and does not include a GUI. All channels, however,
          have KDE Plasma 6 (Wayland) recipes, and it can be added with
          <code>zpkg install plasma-meta</code>.
          See <a href="/en/docs/desktop/">Desktop environment</a> for details.
        </dd>
        <dt>Is installation difficult?</dt>
        <dd>
          It is easier than assembling everything by hand. Unpack stage3, chroot
          into it, and install the kernel and bootloader with
          <code>zpkg install linux grub</code>. Installers for BIOS / UEFI are also available.
        </dd>
        <dt>Which architectures are supported?</dt>
        <dd>x86_64 only. It boots on both BIOS and UEFI.</dd>
        <dt>Does it support Secure Boot?</dt>
        <dd>
          No. GRUB and the kernel are unsigned, so disable Secure Boot before use.
        </dd>
        <dt>How many packages are there?</dt>
        <dd>
          Across the three channels stable / testing / unstable, there are
          {release.recipes.total} recipes in total as of r21
          (stable {release.recipes.channels.stable.core + release.recipes.channels.stable.main + release.recipes.channels.stable.desktop} /
          testing {release.recipes.channels.testing.core + release.recipes.channels.testing.main + release.recipes.channels.testing.desktop} /
          unstable {release.recipes.channels.unstable.core + release.recipes.channels.unstable.main + release.recipes.channels.unstable.desktop}).
          The base is {release.base.top_level} packages, or
          {release.base.closure} with dependencies.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>About the name</h2>
      <p>
        ZIGNOS is a name derived from "<strong>Zenith Innovation for Next-generation Operating Systems</strong>".
      </p>
      <p>
        The tagline is <em>Beyond the Limits.</em>,
        and the catchphrase is <em>Engineered for Performance. Designed for Independence.</em>.
      </p>

      <h2>Read next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/en/docs/install/">Installation on real hardware</a> — installing on BIOS / UEFI</li>
        <li><a href="/en/docs/stage3/">stage3 guide</a> — from unpacking to chroot and kernel installation</li>
        <li><a href="/en/docs/design-pkg/">zpkg design</a> — recipes, DB, USE, transactions</li>
        <li><a href="/en/docs/roadmap/">Roadmap</a> — the M0–M51 record and current status</li>
        <li><a href="/en/about/">Project overview</a> — repository layout and basic information</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="what-cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/en/docs/">Read the documentation</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/en/download/">Download</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
